<section aria-labelledby="floor-h">
  <h2 id="floor-h" class="type-h2">The floor</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Contrast.</strong> 4.5:1 for text, 3:1 for large text and interface parts. Checked in Light and Dark on the <a href="/foundations/color/#contrast-h">color page</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Focus.</strong> A visible focus ring on every interactive element, including on blue and in dark mode. It is 4px with a 2px gap, except on the image tile (see the accepted exceptions below).</li>
    <li><strong>Targets.</strong> Buttons and nav links are 48px tall, form fields are 56px, and text links are at least 32px tall. WCAG 2.2 AA asks for 24px at minimum, and 44px is the AAA level.</li>
    <li><strong>Labels.</strong> Every field has a real <code>&lt;label&gt;</code>. Icon-only buttons have an <code>aria-label</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Color is never the only signal.</strong> Errors add a message and a thicker rule. Current page adds a bar. Links are underlined.</li>
    <li><strong>Keyboard.</strong> A skip link is the first focusable element on every page. Tab order follows reading order.</li>
    <li><strong>Motion.</strong> Respects <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Images.</strong> Informative images carry alt text. Decorative icons are hidden from screen readers.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
<section aria-labelledby="regulated-h">
  <h2 id="regulated-h" class="type-h2">Why this matters for regulated work</h2>
  <p>In healthcare and pharma, required content such as safety information and boxed warnings has to be readable by everyone, and it has to survive review. A system that builds contrast, focus and labeling into the base components means each new page starts from a sound base instead of being fixed at the end.</p>
</section>
<section aria-labelledby="test-h">
  <h2 id="test-h" class="type-h2">How I check</h2>
  <p>Contrast is calculated from the token file on every build. Keyboard and screen-reader behavior is checked by hand on each component before it is documented.</p>
  <p>An automated scan runs with <code>npm run check:a11y</code>. It uses axe-core, a rule checker that reads each page's code in a real browser, on every page in Light and Dark. It also runs three keyboard checks: the skip link is the first Tab stop, the open menu closes when focus leaves it, and the footer's hidden logo link cannot take focus. It is not part of <code>npm run build</code> because it needs a browser. A scan cannot judge everything, so it does not replace a keyboard pass and a screen reader pass by hand.</p>
</section>
<section aria-labelledby="exceptions-h">
  <h2 id="exceptions-h" class="type-h2">Accepted exceptions</h2>
  <p>These are places where the system does not meet the rules above, and I have accepted that. Each has its reason.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Disabled controls.</strong> Disabled text and borders are below 4.5:1 on purpose. WCAG 1.4.3 does not ask inactive controls to meet contrast, and the build's contrast check leaves them out.</li>
    <li><strong>Sideways scrolling at 320px wide.</strong> Data tables, code blocks, the fixed-width preview frames and the image swipe strip scroll sideways inside their own box. WCAG 1.4.10 allows this for content that needs two dimensions, such as data tables and code. The preview frames show a fixed-width layout on purpose. Each one can be reached and scrolled with the keyboard.</li>
    <li><strong>Preview pages opened alone.</strong> The pages under <code>/previews/</code> have no <code>h1</code>, no <code>main</code> landmark and no skip link. They are frames inside component pages, each frame has a title, and they are not in the navigation. They are not meant to be opened alone.</li>
    <li><strong>Repeated landmark names on eight component pages.</strong> Credits, Menu overlay, More case studies, Next case study, Outcome, Section split, Skip link and Top nav each show a live demo that is itself a landmark (a header, a nav, a main or a named section), so the page lists a landmark twice.</li>
    <li><strong>Image tile focus ring.</strong> The image tile's ring is 2px with a 2px gap, not 4px. It is documented on the <a href="/components/image-tile/">Image tile</a> page. It is still clearly visible and keeps at least 3:1 against what is around it.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
